---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import TransformerDiagram from "../components/TransformerDiagram.astro";
import StageStepper from "../components/StageStepper.astro";
import ComputationPanel from "../components/ComputationPanel.astro";
import { TOKENS } from "../lib/toy-example";

const sentence = TOKENS.join(" ");
---

<Base title="The Transformer, one stage at a time">
  <nav class="topbar" aria-label="Primary">
    <a href="./">Home</a>
    <a href="#diagram">Jump to Figure 1</a>
  </nav>

  <main class="prose">
    <h1>The Transformer, one stage at a time</h1>

    <p class="standfirst">
      The encoder half of the Transformer is four ideas stacked on top of one another. This
      page runs all four of them on a single four-word sentence &mdash; <em>{sentence}</em
      > &mdash; and shows you the arithmetic at each step, as it happens.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the encoder has this shape</h2>

    <p>
      Before 2017, a model that read a sentence read it the way you do: one word after
      another, carrying a running summary forward. That works, but it makes distance
      expensive. For the model to relate the first word to the last, information has to
      survive every step in between, and the further apart two words are, the more
      chances there are for the connection to wash out.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Transformer removes the running summary entirely. Every position looks at every
      other position in one operation, and how much any position attends to any other is
      computed from the words themselves. Distance stops being a cost. That single change
      is what the word <em>attention</em> is doing in the title of the paper this page
      follows, <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1706.03762" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >&ldquo;Attention Is All You Need&rdquo;</a
      >.
    </p>

    <p>
      Removing the sequential read has a price: with nothing but a bag of vectors, the
      model has no idea what order the words came in. So order gets added back explicitly,
      as a fixed pattern layered onto each word. What's left is bookkeeping &mdash; a small
      network applied to each position on its own, and the whole arrangement repeated a
      number of times. Those four moves, in that order, are the encoder.
    </p>

    <h2>Step through it</h2>

    <p>
      Pick a stage below. The diagram highlights the block you're inside, and the panel
      beside it does that stage's actual work on the toy sentence. Where a stage has a
      choice to make &mdash; which position, which query word, which attention head &mdash;
      the controls are live, and the numbers move when you change them.
    </p>

    <figure class="figure" id="diagram">
      <div class="figure__card">
        <div class="figure__stage">
          <div class="figure__diagram">
            <TransformerDiagram />
          </div>
          <div class="figure__readout">
            <ComputationPanel />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="figure__controls">
          <StageStepper />
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        <b>Figure 1.</b> The encoder stack, redrawn. On a wide screen the decoder is drawn
        beside it, dimmed and inert: it mirrors this same shape &mdash; masked self-attention,
        attention over the encoder's output, then feed-forward &mdash; but this page has one
        idea, and the idea is the encoder. On a narrow screen the encoder gets the whole
        canvas.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The numbers are real</h2>

    <p>
      Nothing on this page is a screenshot of a model, and nothing is a plausible-looking
      number typed in by hand. There is a small module of pure functions holding a fixed
      set of toy weights, and every figure you see is what those functions return when they
      are run. The attention weights are a genuine softmax over scaled dot products; the
      feed-forward output is a genuine linear&ndash;ReLU&ndash;linear pass. When a number on
      screen changes, it changed because a computation ran again.
    </p>

    <p>
      They are, of course, <em>toy</em> numbers. Four words, four dimensions per vector, two
      dimensions per head, weights that were chosen rather than trained. A real encoder works
      in 512 dimensions and its weights mean something. The mechanism is the same one; the
      scale is not. If you want the same ideas at more length and with better pictures,
      <a href="https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >The Illustrated Transformer</a
      > is the standard next stop. The diagram here is drawn from scratch in its own style
      rather than traced from either source.
    </p>

    <h2>What this leaves out</h2>

    <p>
      The <span class="term">Add &amp; Norm</span> strips are drawn because the architecture
      isn't honest without them, but they aren't steppable &mdash; residual connections and
      layer normalisation are what keep a deep stack trainable, which is a different lesson
      from the one this page is teaching. The eight heads are eight real, separate
      projections, but they are toy projections, so don't read meaning into which head
      prefers which word. And the feed-forward stage is shown operating on a word's raw
      embedding rather than on what attention actually handed it &mdash; the point there is
      the shape of the operation, not the full data path.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .topbar {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    max-width: 72rem;
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-7);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /*
   * Breakout grid: prose sits in the centre track at the reading measure, and
   * anything marked .figure spans all three tracks to escape it.
   */
  .prose {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr min(var(--measure), 100%) 1fr;
    row-gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .prose > * {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  .prose > h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  .standfirst {
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .term {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }

  /*
   * Astro scopes `.prose > *` to `.prose[cid] > [cid]`, which outranks a bare
   * `.figure[cid]` — so the breakout has to be written as a child selector too
   * or the figure stays trapped in the measure column.
   */
  .prose > .figure {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    width: min(72rem, 100%);
    justify-self: center;
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
  }

  .figure__card {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    padding: var(--space-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface-figure);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-figure);
  }

  .figure__controls {
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  figcaption {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: var(--space-4) auto 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /*
   * Below the split, .figure__stage dissolves so the controls can sit between the
   * diagram and the readout: tapping a stage must not push its highlight off-screen.
   * The diagram then sticks to the top of the card while the panel scrolls under it.
   */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .figure__stage {
      display: contents;
    }

    .figure__diagram {
      position: sticky;
      top: 0;
      z-index: 1;
      order: 1;
      padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
      background: var(--surface-figure);
    }

    .figure__diagram :global(svg) {
      height: 42vh;
    }

    .figure__controls {
      order: 2;
    }

    .figure__readout {
      order: 3;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .figure__card {
      padding: var(--space-6);
    }

    .figure__stage {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1.25fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-6);
      align-items: center;
    }
  }
</style>
